## Summary

When a multi-root workspace has **two or more folders** that each define an MCP server with the **same name** in their own `.vscode/mcp.json`, the editor enters a **self-sustaining infinite loop**: the Output → Window log fills continuously with `Overwriting mcp server '<name>' from ... with ...` warnings, alongside repeated `potential listener LEAK detected` and `An unknown error occurred` messages, with no backoff or termination. Any UI that reflects the live MCP server list (e.g. a tool-picker/configuration view) flickers continuously because the registered server set never stabilizes.

## Root cause

`src/vs/workbench/contrib/mcp/browser/mcpWorkbenchService.ts`, `McpWorkbenchService.getEnabledLocalMcpServers()` merges servers **by name only** (scope is not part of the key):

```ts
const result = new Map<string, IWorkbenchLocalMcpServer>();
// ... User scope, then RemoteUser scope ...
for (const server of workspace) {                 // ALL workspace folders
    const existing = result.get(server.name);
    if (existing) {
        this.logService.warn(localize('overwriting',
            "Overwriting mcp server '{0}' from {1} with {2}.",
            server.name, server.mcpResource.path, existing.mcpResource.path));
    }
    result.set(server.name, server);
}
```

The actual defect is `McpWorkbenchService.sort()` in the same file:

```ts
private sort(local: McpWorkbenchServer[]): McpWorkbenchServer[] {
    return local.sort((a, b) => {
        if (a.name === b.name) {
            if (!a.runtimeStatus || a.runtimeStatus.state === Enabled) { return -1; }
            if (!b.runtimeStatus || b.runtimeStatus.state === Enabled) { return 1; }
            return 0;
        }
        return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
    });
}
```

For two entries with the **same name** that are both enabled, this always returns `-1` regardless of argument order — i.e. `cmp(a,b) === -1` **and** `cmp(b,a) === -1`. That is a non-antisymmetric comparator. `Array.prototype.sort` with an inconsistent comparator produces implementation-defined, input-order-dependent permutations.

This service re-sorts (mutating in place) on every server-list change:

```ts
this._register(runOnChange(mcpService.servers, () => {
    this._local = this.sort(this._local);
    this._onChange.fire(undefined);
}));
```

So: re-sort → a different permutation of the same-named entries → `getEnabledLocalMcpServers()` returns a **different winning definition** → the effective server set changes → re-sort is triggered again → … indefinitely.

## Minimal reproduction of the comparator instability (Node)

```js
const cmp = (a, b) => {
  if (a.name === b.name) {
    if (!a.rt || a.rt === 'Enabled') return -1;
    if (!b.rt || b.rt === 'Enabled') return 1;
    return 0;
  }
  return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
};
const arr = [{ name: 'x', id: 'A' }, { name: 'x', id: 'B' }];
for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) { arr.sort(cmp); console.log(arr.map(e => e.id).join('')); }
// AB BA AB BA AB BA  -- flips every call, forever
```

Simulating the full merge (one User-scope entry + two same-named Workspace-scope entries) confirms the winner genuinely alternates every pass, matching the observed log pattern exactly. (A single workspace-folder entry, by contrast, is stable — the loop requires **two or more** same-named workspace-scope entries.)

## Steps to reproduce

1. Open a multi-root workspace with at least two folders.
2. In two of the folders, add a `.vscode/mcp.json` that each define an MCP server under the **identical name** (contents/command can differ).
3. Reload/open the window with an extension that surfaces MCP servers active (e.g. an AI chat extension with MCP support).
4. Observe the Window output log.

## Expected

Either:
- a single, stable winner is chosen deterministically and the log emits the `Overwriting` warning once, or
- duplicate names across scopes are explicitly rejected/flagged without continuous churn.

## Actual

An unbounded loop: continuous `Overwriting mcp server` warnings alternating winners, `potential listener LEAK detected` warnings, and repeated `An unknown error occurred` messages. Any dependent UI (tool configuration list, server status indicators) flickers indefinitely. Only remedy found: remove the duplicate name from all but one scope.

## Suggested fixes (any one would resolve it)

1. Use a stable/antisymmetric comparator — e.g. break ties for equal names using a stable secondary key (scope id, resource path) instead of unconditionally returning `-1`.
2. De-duplicate by name **before** sorting, so the comparator is never asked to order two same-named entries in the first place — `getEnabledLocalMcpServers()` already does this collapsing; doing it earlier (in `_local` itself) would remove the ambiguity at the source.
3. At minimum, skip firing the change event when the resulting enabled/effective server set is unchanged in content (independent of array order) — this alone would break the loop even without fixing the sort's determinism.

